Multiple Myeloma
Haematology and Oncology
Severe unremitting bone pain/pathological fractures, Unexplained progressive renal failure, Hypercalcaemia/anaemia, >10 percent clonal plasma cells on bone marrow biopsy

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: HMO-032
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of unremitting, severe lower back pain following trivial movement, preventing all ambulation and requiring urgent assessment for spinal cord compression.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an elderly patient who experienced a sudden, agonizing crack in their lower back while bending to pick up a shoe, rendering them entirely unable to walk. Urgent in-person evaluation was necessary to rule out acute spinal cord compression and manage severe, uncontrolled bone pain, as leaving the patient immobilized overnight posed a high risk of permanent neurological deficit.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "I bent over to pick up my slipper, felt a massive crunch in my spine, and now the pain is so deep and agonizing I cannot move my legs to stand."
History of Present Illness: The 72-year-old patient reports an insidious 3-month history of profound generalized fatigue, systemic malaise, and an unexplained unintentional weight loss of 5 kg. Over the past month, they noted progressive, deep, dull, and severe unremitting bone pain localizing to the lower back and ribs. One hour ago, following trivial trauma (bending over), they experienced a sudden, sharp exacerbation of spinal pain preventing weight-bearing. They also endorse recent frothy urine and profound lethargy, with a history of recurrent pneumonia treated twice this year.
Functional Status: Bedbound and rigidly guarding the spine; entirely unable to transfer.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; unable to access the bathroom or telephone if dropped.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 145/90 | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 96 percent | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Pale, lethargic adult in visible agony, lying completely flat and resisting any torso movement.
Physical Exam:
Musculoskeletal: Exquisite, localized bony tenderness upon direct palpation of the L3 and L4 spinous processes, highly suggestive of a pathological fragility fracture. Marked tenderness also elicited on palpation of the left lower ribs.
Neurological: Cranial nerves intact. Sensation to light touch and pinprick remains intact in both lower extremities. Motor power is 4/5 bilaterally, limited primarily by severe pain rather than true paraparesis. Deep tendon reflexes are 2+ and symmetrical. No loss of bowel or bladder tone.
Skin: Unexplained spontaneous bruising noted on the bilateral forearms. Marked pallor of the conjunctivae.
Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, regular rhythm.
Environmental Observations: Slip-on shoes on the floor where the incident occurred.
Medication Reconciliation: Took two paracetamol 1 hour ago with absolute zero efficacy.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Multiple Myeloma - Status: Exacerbated (Suspected Pathological Vertebral Fracture)
Differentials:

Acute Spinal Cord Compression - Ruled out/unlikely due to: preserved bilateral lower limb sensation, symmetrical reflexes, and absence of bowel or bladder incontinence, though the severe fracture puts them at immediate impending risk.

Osteoporotic Compression Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the profound systemic signs of anaemia, unexplained recurrent pneumonia, and spontaneous bruising, which point to severe bone marrow infiltration rather than isolated benign bone loss.

Metastatic Prostate Cancer - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the specific clinical picture aligning with plasma cell dyscrasia (frothy urine suggesting renal tubular damage) and the presence of recurrent bacterial infections due to hypogammaglobulinaemia.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient presents with an acute, agonizing spinal event following trivial trauma, superimposed on a subacute history of deep bone pain, fatigue, spontaneous bruising, and frothy urine. This constellation is highly suspicious for a pathological vertebral fracture secondary to undiagnosed Multiple Myeloma, with likely coexisting renal failure and anaemia. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would leave the patient in unmanageable agony and exposed to the imminent risk of the collapsed vertebra shifting and causing catastrophic, irreversible spinal cord transection. The urgent home visit was critically necessary to establish neurological stability, provide potent immediate analgesia, and facilitate strict spinal precautions during emergency ambulance transfer.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Administered immediate intramuscular morphine 5mg for severe bone pain. Implemented strict bed rest with log-rolling only. Immediate dispatch of a priority ambulance for transfer to the Emergency Department for a skeletal survey (anticipating punched-out lytic bone lesions), serum protein electrophoresis, and urgent neurosurgical/oncological review.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Remain completely flat on your back and do not attempt to sit up or turn without paramedic assistance. By keeping the spine perfectly aligned, you prevent the fractured bone fragments from shifting and damaging your spinal cord.

Focus on slow, deep breaths despite the rib pain. By actively inflating the lower lungs, you prevent the tiny airways from collapsing, reducing your immediate risk of developing another severe chest infection.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Immediate ED transfer overrides routine GP follow-up.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Immediate ambulance transfer is required. If the patient suddenly loses all feeling in their legs, develops sharp shooting pain down the legs, or loses control of their bladder while waiting, inform the dispatch operator immediately.

